From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 20:42:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(deploy): route parity against production, and the branch
 rules that make it unnecessary
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

For a week the live site served a build two features old while everything in
this repository reported success. `pnpm build` said 331 pages. Sixty-one guards
passed. Two hundred and fifty-six tests passed. `vercel --prod` said Ready and
Aliased. And /quote-check — live and confirmed 200 hours earlier — returned 404
to every visitor and every crawler.

`vercel inspect ecowoods.ca` named a deployment whose aliases included
…-git-main-…: a Git-integration build from `main`. Production deployed from
`main`, the site lived on `floor-graph`, and patches were being uploaded to
`main` through the web UI — so every patch upload triggered a production build
of a branch that did not contain the site and took the alias back within minutes
of each CLI deploy. Re-pointing the alias by hand held for four minutes.

Nothing in the repository could see any of that, because everything in the
repository was correct. That is the shape of the whole class: a pipeline can
report success and serve something else, and the only way to know is to ask the
live host what it actually has.

verify-live.sh already proves a sample, and a sample is exactly what let this run
for a week. scripts/verify-live-routes.mjs proves all of it: it walks
apps/web/app for pages AND route handlers — the /md twins and the machine files
are handlers, forty-nine URLs that exist precisely so machines can read them and
that a page-only walk cannot see — expands every dynamic segment from the same
manifests that generate it, adds every GET endpoint in the API manifest, and
fetches all 103. A route that exists here and 404s there names itself.

Two bugs found while writing it, both in the enumeration, which is the half that
goes wrong quietly: reading lib/seo-data.ts for `slug:` returns the SERVICES
list, so the first run asked production for /service-areas/hardwood-installation;
and walking only page.tsx made every markdown twin invisible. `--list` prints the
target set without touching the network, which is how both were caught.

It is not in `pnpm verify` — it reaches the network, and a guard that reports a
proxy's 403 as a broken site is a guard people learn to ignore. It runs after a
deploy, which is the only moment it can tell you anything.

docs/DEPLOY.md states the rules that make it a backstop rather than a necessity:
one branch is the site, production deploys from it through the Git integration,
nothing reaches it except a merged pull request, and patches go to the orphan
`patches` branch — a .patch on the production branch triggers a production build
and lands at the root of every PR merge commit, where verify:hygiene fails it.

